{
  "dom_id": "step-q312658",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}={x}^{x}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+{sec}^{2}⁡(x))$",
  "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =(x)^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′}+{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
